(foreach-syntax)=

## `foreach` Statement


A string can supply characters to a loop. For these introductory examples, use ordinary English letters, digits, and punctuation. `text.Length` is the number of UTF-16 code units, and `text[i]` reads one `char` at zero-based index `i`. The valid indexes run from zero through `text.Length - 1`; an empty string has length zero and no valid index. Some Unicode characters require more than one `char`.

A counted loop visits the positions:

```csharp
string text = "ABC";
for (int i = 0; i < text.Length; i++)
{
    Console.WriteLine(text[i]);
}
```

A `foreach` visits the values without managing an index:

```csharp
string text = "ABC";
foreach (char letter in text)
{
    Console.WriteLine(letter);
}
```

Both print A, B, and C on separate lines. `foreach` is useful when each value receives the same treatment; an indexed loop is useful when positions or reverse order matter. Strings are immutable sequences, not arrays. Arrays and other collections follow in Chapters 7-8. Cleaning, searching, and extracting text are developed in Chapter 5; none is required here.
Continue with [Factorial and simulation practice](assignments/additional_practice.ipynb).

```{index} break in foreach
```

## `break` in `foreach`


Stop when a matching character is found. Each variable is declared in this standalone example:

```csharp
string text = "INV-204";
char target = '-';
bool found = false;
foreach (char letter in text)
{
    if (letter == target)
    {
        found = true;
        break;
    }
}
Console.WriteLine(found ? "Target found" : "Target not found");
```

Change `text` to an empty string or to `INV204` and predict the result. The `while` lesson follows in this chapter.

```{index} Do-While Loops
```

(do-while)=

## Read and Trace the Do-While Syntax


The general form of a {{ do_while }} statement (referred to as the “`do` statement” in
the C# language reference [^0403_while_statement-1]) is:

```csharp
initialization

do
{
  // statement(s)
  // iterator expression
} while ( condition );

```
The `do-while` loop is a variation of the `while loop`. The `do` statement executes a statement or a block of statements, followed by a Boolean expression to decide the continuation of the iteration. Because that expression is evaluated **after** each execution of the loop, a do loop executes **one or more** times. The `do` loop differs from the *while* loop, which executes *zero or more* times.

Compare the `do-while` general syntax with the `while` loop below and you should see clearly the difference in logic.
```csharp
initialization

while ( *condition* )
{
  // statement(s)
  // iterator expression
}
```
In a `while` loop, if the condition evaluates to false from
the very beginning, the body of statements will not be executed at all.
In a `do-while` statement, the body statement(s) is *always* executed at least
once, then the iteration continues if the followed condition tested true.
This logic can be seen in the flowchart:
```{figure} ../../figures/flowchart-do-while-loop.jpg
:alt: flowchart do-while loop
:width: 250
Flowchart of a do-while loop.
```
As a comparison, the while and do-while loops differ in several ways:
| Feature | while | do-while |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Condition | checked before body statement is executed. | checked after body statement is executed. |
| Body statement | may be executed zero times. | is executed at least once. |
| Semicolon | no semicolon at the end of loop header: `while(condition)` | semicolon at the end of loop header: `while(condition);` |
| Loop Variable | initialized before the execution of loop. | initialized before or within the loop. |
| Control | entry-controlled. | exit-controlled. |
| Syntax | `while(condition) { statement(s); }` | `do { statement(s); } while(condition);` |

To get familiar with the do-while loop syntax, practice the following code with `csharprepl`, `Run C#`, or VS Code:

```csharp
int n = 0;
do
{
  Console.Write(n);
  n++;
} while (n < 5);

// output: 01234
```
```{index} do-while Example: Right Triangle
```

```csharp
int a, b, c;
do {
    Console.WriteLine("Think of integer sides for a right triangle.");
    Console.Write("Enter integer leg: ");
    a = int.Parse(Console.ReadLine());
    Console.Write("Enter another integer leg: ");
    b = int.Parse(Console.ReadLine());
    Console.Write("Enter integer hypotenuse: ");
    c = int.Parse(Console.ReadLine());
    if (a*a + b*b != c*c) {
        Console.WriteLine("Not a right triangle: Try again!");
    }
} while (a*a + b*b != c*c);
```

(gap-validate-a-number-before-using-it)=
## Validate a Number Before Using It

```{index} gap revision: validate a number before using it
```
An order desk must distinguish text that is not a number from a number that violates a business rule. Parsing answers the first question; a comparison answers the second. `int.Parse` assumes valid text and can throw. `int.TryParse` reports ordinary conversion failure as a Boolean so a decision or loop can respond without exception handling.

General call pattern (schematic):

```csharp
int quantity;
bool converted = int.TryParse(text, out quantity);
```

`text` is the input string. `out quantity` identifies a variable the library method fills. You are calling an existing method, not defining one. It is safe to read `quantity` afterward, but use it as an accepted order quantity only when conversion succeeds and the range check passes. On conversion failure this overload writes zero, which does not mean the customer entered zero. Chapter 5 explains how to define your own output parameters.

| Input | Conversion | Range 1 through 20 | Response |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `"many"` | False | Not evaluated as an order | Ask again. |
| `"0"` | True | False | Ask again. |
| `"12"` | True | True | Accept 12. |
| Missing input (`null`) | False | Not accepted | End input rather than retry forever. |

Read the trace across each row: conversion is necessary but insufficient. The loop below checks conversion before range and stops on end of input. Type `many`, then `0`, then `12` to follow the first three rows. It prints two rejection messages and accepts 12. This interactive listing is a VS Code application; its result depends on the supplied input.

```csharp
using System;
int quantity = 0;
while (true)
{
    Console.Write("Quantity (1-20): ");
    string? text = Console.ReadLine();
    if (text == null) break;
    if (int.TryParse(text, out quantity) && quantity >= 1 && quantity <= 20)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Accepted {quantity}");
        break;
    }
    Console.WriteLine("Enter a whole number from 1 through 20.");
}
```

A common mistake is to call `Parse` first and try to validate afterward: invalid text fails before the comparison runs. Another is retrying forever after `ReadLine` returns null. A sentinel such as `quit` is a third policy: test it before attempting conversion.

See the language/API reference for this contract.[^gap-validation]
**Guided practice.** Predict conversion and acceptance for `"20"`, `"21"`, `"2.5"`, and `""`, then change `text` in the standalone example. Explain why the empty string and zero need different feedback. Extend the interactive loop to accept `quit` without accepting it as a quantity.
```{rubric} Footnotes
```

[^gap-validation]: [Microsoft: Int32.TryParse](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.int32.tryparse).
[^0403_while_statement-1]: For an introduction of the while loop, see: [ the while loop in C# Language Reference](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/language-specification/statements#1392-the-while-statement)

There are several ways to code the shorter Euclidean algorithm at the
beginning of this GCD section. It is a repetitive pattern, and a loop
can be used. There are two parameters, `a` and `b`, to the gcd, and they
can be successively changed, suggesting a loop. What is the continuation
condition? You stop when `b` is 0, so you continue while `b != 0`. The
parameters `a` and `b` need to be replaced by `b` and `a % b`. One extra
variable needs to be introduced to make this double change work. The
simplest is to introduce a variable `r` for the remainder. Check and see
for yourself that you need an extra variable like `r`:

    /// Return the greatest common divisor of nonnegative numbers,
    /// not both 0.
    public static int GreatestCommonDivisor (int a, int b)
    {
       while (b != 0) {
          int r = a % b;
          a = b;
          b = r;
       }
       return a;
    } 

More verbose demonstration code, that prints the progress each time
through loop is in `g_c_d_remainder_loop/g_c_d_remainder_loop.cs`.

```{index} Method Calls & Returns
```

## Method Calls & Returns


The runnable cell calls MyMethod from top-level statements. The retained class-based version makes the same call inside Main. The call is MyMethod(), not a call to Main.

When making a method call, you use the method **name** followed by
**parentheses**. The method header `static void MyMethod()` indicates the return type is `void` and there are no formal parameters, which means you can call it simply by writing `MyMethod();`.

An important feature of functions/methods is that they can **return** values to their
**callers**. In the example below, `SquareTheNumber(int num)` is defined with a return type of `int`
and is called directly from the top-level code, passing a parameter.
::::{dropdown} Class-based console-project version
The top-level example above is the version to run as a standalone notebook cell. This complete project version retains the explicit `Main` structure. Save it as the only program in a separate console project; do not paste it after top-level statements in the same file.

```csharp
using System;

class Program {
static int SquareTheNumber(int num)
{
    return num * num; // the value to be returned is given by the expression in the return statement.
}

static void Main(){
int digit = 4;
int squaredNum = SquareTheNumber(digit);
int squaredAndSummed = squaredNum + SquareTheNumber(digit);

Console.WriteLine(squaredNum);
Console.WriteLine(squaredAndSummed);
Console.WriteLine(SquareTheNumber(5));
}
}
```
::::
In this example:

- `return num * num;` computes the value that replaces this call in the caller.
- `digit` starts at 4. Passing it binds the value 4 to `num`.
- `squaredNum` is a variable, not a method; it stores the returned 16.
- The addition uses `squaredNum` plus a second call with digit still equal to 4: 16 + 16 = 32.
- The final call uses 5 and returns 25. The three printed values are 16, 32, and 25.

The return type must agree with the returned expression through an allowed conversion. `void` means no returned value; it does not mean the method cannot print or have another effect.
